<!doctype html>
<!--
  EDITING GUIDE
  1. Edit the text directly. Style block is byte-identical to janardan-profile.html: do not add classes or CSS.
  2. Every bullet keeps the shape Action, then Scale, then Business result.
  3. Print: Chrome > Print > Destination "Save as PDF" > Paper A4 > Margins "Default" > Background graphics ON. Target is exactly 2 pages.
  4. If text spills onto page 3, trim the oldest bullets first (Earlier Career).
  5. Claim scope that must not drift: the CloudIQ team shipped through the organization's existing pipeline and owned
     release discipline, upgrade compatibility and observability. It did not build the pipeline. No platform-engineering claim.
-->
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="description" content="Janardan Revuru, engineering leader in application development, cloud and delivery: Java, application programming interfaces and web at scale, teams built 2 to 100, AI-assisted engineering across 600 engineers.">
  <title>Janardan Revuru: Director of Engineering | Application Development, Cloud, DevOps and Delivery at Scale</title>
  <style>
      :root {
        color-scheme: light;
        --ink: #172234;
        --muted: #4d5a6b;
        --accent: #183b63;
        --rule: #cbd4df;
        --paper: #fff;
        --canvas: #eef2f6;
      }
      * { box-sizing: border-box; }
      html { background: var(--canvas); }
      body {
        margin: 0;
        color: var(--ink);
        background: var(--canvas);
        font: 11pt/1.5 Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
      }
      main {
        width: min(900px, 100% - 32px);
        margin: 24px auto;
        padding: 42px 54px;
        background: var(--paper);
      }
      h1 { margin: 0 0 5px; font-size: 25pt; line-height: 1.15; }
      .headline { margin: 0 0 12px; color: var(--accent); font-size: 12.5pt; font-weight: 700; }
      .contact { margin: 0; font-size: 10.5pt; line-height: 1.7; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
      .contact a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
      section { margin-top: 22px; }
      h2 {
        margin: 0 0 9px;
        padding-bottom: 5px;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
        color: var(--accent);
        font-size: 13pt;
        line-height: 1.25;
      }
      h3 { margin: 13px 0 3px; font-size: 11pt; line-height: 1.35; }
      p { margin: 0 0 7px; }
      ul { margin: 5px 0 10px; padding-left: 21px; }
      li { margin: 4px 0; padding-left: 1px; }
      .group { font-weight: 700; margin: 12px 0 3px; color: var(--ink); }
      .url { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
      a { color: var(--accent); }
      .tl-row { margin: 3px 0; }
      .tl-co { font-weight: 700; }
      @media (max-width: 640px) {
        main { width: 100%; margin: 0; padding: 28px 22px; }
      }
      @page { size: A4; margin: 12mm; }
      @media print {
        html, body { width: auto; background: #fff; }
        body { color: #111; font-size: 9.2pt; line-height: 1.32; }
        main { width: auto; max-width: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
        h1 { font-size: 20pt; }
        .headline { font-size: 10.5pt; }
        .contact { font-size: 9pt; }
        section { margin-top: 10px; }
        h2 { margin-bottom: 4px; font-size: 11pt; break-after: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; }
        section { break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; }
        h3 { margin-top: 6px; font-size: 9.5pt; break-after: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; }
        p, li { orphans: 3; widows: 3; }
        ul { margin: 2px 0 5px; padding-left: 18px; }
        li { margin: 1px 0; }
        .group { margin: 7px 0 2px; }
        .tl-row { margin: 1px 0; }
        a { color: #111; text-decoration: none; }
        .skills { margin-top: 10px; }
        .skills p { margin-bottom: 2px; }
      }
    </style>

  <style>
    /* Print-fit delta for this page only. The style block above is byte-identical to janardan-profile.html
       and keeps its section{break-inside:avoid}; that rule pushes the whole Experience section to page 2
       and leaves page 1 half empty. Here sections flow, while rows, list items and headings stay intact. */
    @media print {
      body { font-size: 9pt; line-height: 1.29; }
      section { break-inside: auto; page-break-inside: auto; }
      .tl-row, li { break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; }
      h3 { break-after: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
  <h1>Janardan Revuru</h1>
  <p class="headline">Engineering Leader &middot; Application Development, Cloud and Delivery Discipline &middot; Teams Built 2 to 100</p>
  <p class="contact">
    Bengaluru, India &nbsp; +91 8050780608 &nbsp; janardan.revuru@gmail.com<br>
    LinkedIn: linkedin.com/in/rjanardan &nbsp; Portfolio: https://janalogy.com
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Professional Summary</h2>
    <p>Engineering director with 30 years in enterprise software: 15 in networking, data and telecom, 5 in server high availability, and 10 in storage and data protection. Grew the India function from 2 to 100 through hiring, and led 5 leaders and up to 60 engineers across 3 sites with global reporting. Shipped three version-1 enterprise products. Proved AI-assisted engineering at 60 engineers, then led it across a 600-engineer, multi-geography business unit by influence for about 7 years, an additional responsibility of about 30 percent of the role. Brings application development depth in Java, web technologies and PostgreSQL; cloud lifecycle across Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud; and development and operations (DevOps) practice carried through an established continuous integration and continuous delivery pipeline, with production observability, and consistently top-decile team engagement.</p>
    <p><strong>Open to:</strong> Director, Senior Director and Head of Engineering roles leading multi-team delivery in application development, cloud and infrastructure software.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Selected Outcomes</h2>
    <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-co">3 version-1 products</span> &middot; shipped at Dell, HPE and Tasman Networks</div>
    <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-co">60 then 600 engineers</span> &middot; AI-assisted engineering proved inside the line, then across the business unit by influence: 3x productivity, 30% higher engagement</div>
    <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-co">2 to 100 engineers</span> &middot; built with 2% attrition against a 7% organization average</div>
    <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-co">85% say/do ratio</span> &middot; up from 50%, with velocity up 2x to 5x</div>
    <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-co">Continuous delivery and observability</span> &middot; CloudIQ monitoring service: release discipline on an established pipeline, production telemetry, 40% of systems in the field</div>
    <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-co">80% faster time to resolve</span> &middot; support cases down 50%</div>
    <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-co">3 cloud provider deployments</span> &middot; Data Domain Virtual Edition lifecycle management on Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud</div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Core Strengths</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Application development: Java, web technologies (Angular, TypeScript), application programming interfaces (REST, OpenAPI, Redfish), PostgreSQL and SQLite</li>
      <li>Cloud and cloud-native delivery: Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, Google Cloud; Kubernetes-based distributed products</li>
      <li>Continuous delivery: shipping on an established continuous integration and continuous delivery pipeline, release discipline, upgrade compatibility across versions</li>
      <li>Observability: product monitoring, alerting and field telemetry in a software-as-a-service monitoring platform</li>
      <li>Organization building and talent: 2 to 100 engineers, 5 leaders, global reporting and one-team culture</li>
      <li>Management and supportability software: the management plane of enterprise systems</li>
      <li>Storage, backup and recovery appliances: supportability, serviceability and SMI-S</li>
      <li>Roadmap, release and customer escalation ownership</li>
      <li>Scaled Agile, delivery governance, and AI-assisted engineering at 600-engineer scale</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Experience</h2>

    <h3>Director of Engineering, Dell Technologies, Aug 2021 to Sep 2026</h3>
    <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-co">Previously</span> &middot; Senior Manager, Dell Technologies, Dec 2016 to Jul 2021</div>
    <p><em>Line responsibility: 5 leaders (senior managers, architects, product owners) and up to 60 engineers across 3 sites with global reporting; owned management and supportability software for PowerProtect DD and for a distributed Kubernetes-based data protection product with 5 early-preview customers. The application stack for the management software in the Senior Manager period was Java, web front ends and application programming interfaces, with SQLite embedded storage, PostgreSQL, and a time-series extension for metrics. Built the India function to 100 through hiring. Additional responsibility, about 30 percent of the role: led Agile and AI transformation across a 600-engineer, multi-geography business unit for about 7 years, 540 of them by influence rather than line authority, working with product management, design, support and engineering leaders.</em></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Built the organization.</strong> Grew the India function from 2 to 100 through hiring, with global reporting and one-team culture; hired and coached user experience, control path and security talent; held top-decile engagement scores, with attrition of 2% against a 7% organization average.</li>
      <li><strong>Proved AI-assisted engineering at 60 engineers first.</strong> Moved the 60 engineers and 5 leaders in the line from AI-assisted coding to spec-driven development on an AI harness, and defined the adoption metrics that later governed the wider rollout: installation, weekly and daily use, and code-generation effectiveness per team.</li>
      <li><strong>Then scaled it across the business unit.</strong> Moved about 600 engineers, 540 of them outside the line, from AI-assisted coding to agentic workflows using the same harness: 3x productivity and 30% higher engagement. Worked with IT to fine-tune the integrated development environment and on-premise code models to a million-line codebase.</li>
      <li><strong>Delivered product faster.</strong> Used AI to bring graphical interface delivery for a Kubernetes data protection product forward by 6 months; the early build went to Technical Marketing for previews with 5 customers, whose feedback reprioritized work before Beta and enabled customer demonstrations ahead of plan.</li>
      <li><strong>Delivered a software-as-a-service product on a continuous delivery cadence.</strong> Led integration with CloudIQ, later repositioned as AIOps (artificial intelligence for information technology operations), reaching 40% of systems in the field: AI-driven insight, observability, telemetry and continuous upgrades. Team responsibility was release discipline, upgrade compatibility and production observability, delivered through the organization's existing continuous integration and continuous delivery pipeline.</li>
      <li><strong>Cut support cost and time to resolve.</strong> Delivered supportability and serviceability for the PowerProtect DD backup and recovery appliance across serviceability, security, REST APIs, graphical interface and observability, reducing support cases by 50% and mean time to resolve by 80%.</li>
      <li><strong>Opened cloud as a delivery channel.</strong> Led API-based lifecycle management for Data Domain Virtual Edition in AWS, Azure and Google Cloud, reaching 25 cloud deployments.</li>
      <li><strong>Modernized the stack.</strong> Moved the Data Protection web stack from Google Web Toolkit to Angular, shortening the interface release cycle by 30% and reducing interface defects by 50%.</li>
      <li><strong>Changed how the company delivers.</strong> Led Dell's Agile transformation three times, an additional responsibility over about 7 years held by influence rather than line authority: waterfall to Scrum, Scrum@Scale, then across the Infrastructure Solutions Group. Certified 100% of employees and leaders and ran portfolio-level executive Scrum events. Earned the Senior Vice President's Game Changer award. Velocity increased 2x to 5x and say/do predictability improved from 50% to 85%.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Engineering Manager, Hewlett Packard Enterprise, Mar 2011 to Dec 2016</h3>
    <p><em>Managed three teams building management software for the HPE high-availability clustering product for business-critical applications, and coordinated a 25-engineer project team on clustering and disaster recovery for HP-UX.</em></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lowered customers' operating cost.</strong> Supervised the architecture and implementation of a multi-cluster manager on a modern technology stack (Java, application programming interfaces, web front end, PostgreSQL), which reduced customers' overall operational cost by 20%.</li>
      <li><strong>Protected mission-critical applications.</strong> Gave management oversight to the disaster recovery program, working closely with EMC as storage partner, and delivered a disaster recovery solution for short and long distance clusters, with 3 greenfield deployments.</li>
      <li><strong>Set direction on a strategic platform.</strong> Gave technical oversight to the HP OneView Network module, part of the strategic HPE composable infrastructure built on a new software stack.</li>
      <li><strong>Built a high-performing team.</strong> Transformed three teams into a high-performing group with higher employee engagement.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Earlier Career, 1996 to 2011</h3>
    <p>Nortel and Avaya (2007 to 2011): led EMEA customer support engineering for an access router repurposed and rebranded as a secure router. Motorola (2005 to 2007): Engineering Manager for the Operations Management Center covering GSM. Tasman Networks (2003 to 2005): delivered a version-1 router element manager in 9 months, including hiring a new Bangalore team. Siemens Technology India (2001 to 2003): technical leader for GSM 2.5G features including location update and lawful interception. Wipro and Amber Networks (1996 to 2001): network management and edge-router management software.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Education and Certifications</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>PhD in Artificial Intelligence</strong>, REVA University, part-time, expected June 2028</li>
      <li><strong>Master of Technology in Data Science and Artificial Intelligence</strong>, BITS Pilani, 2024</li>
      <li><strong>Bachelor of Engineering in Computer Science</strong>, Vijayanagar Engineering College; <strong>Master of Business Administration in Marketing</strong>, Indira Gandhi National Open University</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Certifications: Project Management Professional (PMP) &middot; Certified Scrum Master &middot; SAFe Agilist &middot; Six Sigma Green Belt &middot; Certified Financial Manager</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Patents, Recognition and Community</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>US 20220337596 and US 20240244058: adaptive-trust authentication and authorization (granted)</li>
      <li>US 20240354154: parameter-based adaptive scheduling of jobs (granted)</li>
      <li>US 20260203081 A1: design-system-aware interface validation (published)</li>
      <li>Dell Senior Vice President Game Changer award &middot; LEAD program, top 2% of leaders</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Founder of technology communities with 22K members and 100+ events in 11 years (Google, Microsoft, Dell, HPE, Nutanix and IBM among others). Academic Council, Madanapalle Institute of Technology and Science; Board of Studies, Kishkindha University; mentor to employees pursuing the BITS Pilani Master of Technology.</p>
  </section>
</main>
</body>
</html>
